<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq</h1><p class="st-meta">1118 CE – 1194 CE · capital Hamadan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western branch of the Great Seljuks, ruling western Iran and Iraq from Hamadan while real power slipped to their atabegs and the caliphs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad I Tapar</a> died in 1118, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan">Mahmud II</a> succeeded to the western lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, while his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Sanjar</a> kept Khurasan as senior sultan. The western sultanate, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, covered '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Persian Iraq</a>' (the Jibal), <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans depended on Turkish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who served as atabegs (guardians) of young princes and often held real power, most notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eldiguzids">Eldiguzids</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mas'ud defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustarshid">al-Mustarshid</a>, who was murdered in 1135, but the Abbasid caliphs gradually regained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii">Muhammad II</a>'s siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1157 failed. Persian court culture and patronage continued, with poets and historians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril-iii">Tughril III</a> tried to free himself from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eldiguzids">Eldiguzids</a>, but he was killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Ray</a> in 1194 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-tekish">Tekish</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, ending Seljuk rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1118 CE – 1194 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.95%;width:1.51%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1118 CE – 1194 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hamadan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahmud II, Mas'ud, Tughril III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seljuks of Iraq, Seljuks of Hamadan, Iraqi Seljuks</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1156&amp;focus=seljuk-sultanate-of-iraq" aria-label="Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5911 918 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="